 "observations": [
  "The page number «164» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the left the number of the table, «Tab. 53», in bold sans serif, underlined. The page has no signature. The titles (L01-L04), in bold, the sigla in roman: N's title, O's «Elotari», C's «Tadil avtarid elketib»; L04, «Locus1) draconis eius 0. 21. 10», in smaller bold type, followed by «(CON)». The folios of the manuscripts stand at the left of L03 and L04, «C 69r, O 126v,» and «N 34v.», the letters raised; here as left margin notes.",
  "The table: rules and heads as on p. 163 (JP2 0195), with «Latitudo Mercurii accedentis5)» over «prima» and «secunda». Here each head is set from the top, one printed line to a line (L05-L09), the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns. L10, the units, in bold.",
  "L11-L40. Checked as on p. 162 (JP2 0194): the Sublimatio is 7s 14° 54′ less half the Examinatio argumenti in every row, a half minute counted as a whole, except at 67° (L17), where 7s 6° 40′ stands for 7s 6° 40½′. The Latitudo secunda is 6° 15′ · sin x to within 0.9″ in every row, and 6° 15′ 0″, the greatest latitude of Mercury, at 90°.",
  "L11-L40, the Examinatio argumenti. The radius that reproduces each value as the equation of an epicycle is between 21.97 and 22.04 in every row; against the epicycle of radius 21.97 of p. 162 (JP2 0194) the table is 0.1′ to 2.9′ larger (90°: 20° 9′ for 20° 6.7′). The steps of the column fall from 14′ to 7′ a degree; the greatest equation of an epicycle of radius 22, about 21° 30′, falls at about 111½°.",
  "L11-L40, the other columns: the Examinatio centri increases by 0′ to 2′ a degree to 4° 2′ at 90°, its greatest value; from 1° to 60° (pp. 162-163, JP2 0194-0195) it lies within 1.6′ below and 2.9′ above 3° 56′ · sin x, from 61° on 0.2′ to 6.0′ above it, and up to 5.4′ below 4° 2′ · sin x. The Statio prima decreases from 4s 25° 16′ to 4s 24° 38′, by 1′ to 3′ a degree to 71° and then by 1′ a degree (0′ from 88° to 89°). The Latitudo prima decreases from 1° 33′ at 61° by 1′ every second or third degree to 1° 21′ at 89° and 90°.",
  "Notes on the readings. Note a: the last argument, 3s 0°, is N's; C and O have 2. Checked: the Examinatio argumenti 15° 27′ at 62° (note c, O and N, against C's 17), 18° 7′ at 76° (note d, the edition's 7 against the 50 of C and O; the note does not say «correxi») and C's 19° 13′ at 83° (note e, against O's 14 and N's 22) agree with the Sublimatio of their rows, 7s 7° 11′, 7s 5° 51′ and 7s 5° 18′; its degrees 20 at 89° and 90° (notes b, O and N, against C's 19) with the epicycle; the degrees 4 of the Examinatio centri from 88° to 90° (notes f, O and N, against C's 3) with its column; O's 1° 30′ of the Latitudo prima at 70° (note g, against C's 29) with its column.",
  "L15, the Examinatio argumenti at 65°: a speck before the «3»; L21, the Statio prima at 71°: a dark speck at the upper left of the «4» of the signs; neither is transcribed. Italics: «om.». L33, the sign «8» of the second argument at 83°: see the critical note."
 ],
